Shopify Global Accounts Business Review 
BIDS Build Guide & Operating Requirements
Strategic Alignment - CARL
The Shopify Global Accounts in TaskUs will be governed through a four-pillar strategic framework–CARL–aligned with TaskUs core values and Shopify’s operating principles. 

CARL translates shared expectations around excellence, ownership, people, adaptability, merchant focus, continuous improvement and innovation into a practical governance system for how we measure performance, make decisions, execute actions, and institutionalize what works. 
CONSISTENT
Deliver excellence reliably through disciplined standards and execution.
Business Signals:
Performance: Exceeds expectations and leads the vendor network. All sites MUST occupy the top 4 spots in ALL measured business outcomes. 
Quality: Delivers world class quality interactions that delight merchants. Minimal to zero instances of critical errors, ZTPs, and security incidents. 
Execution: Operates critically on routines. Standards and systems are followed reliably and consistently. 
Variation: Reduces unexplained performance variations. 
Client Confidence: Delivers commitments and results predictably. 

Governance question: Can we consistently deliver the standards we expect?
ACCOUNTABLE
Own outcomes, follow through on commitments, and surface risks early.
Business Signals:
Ownership: Every material gap and action has a clear owner.
Execution: Commitments and milestones are delivered on time. 
Action Effectiveness: Actions demonstrate observable impact.
Escalation: Risks are surfaced early and managed at the right level. 
Governance: Decisions, actions, and outcomes are visible and traceable. 

Governance question: Are we taking ownership of what the business needs? 
RESILIENT
Protect our people and capacity while adapting quickly to change.
Business Signals:
People Health: Attrition, shrinkage, sentiment and capability remain healthy. 
Capacity: Staffing and operational capacity can absorb demand and change.
Risk: Compliance, security, and operational risks are controlled.
Adaptability: Teams respond effectively to changes in volume, process, product, and policy. 
Recovery: Performance recovers quickly following disruption. 

Governance question: Can the team absorb change and continue to perform? 
LOCKED-IN
Turn proven improvements into sustainable, scalable ways of working. 
Business Signals:
Standardization: Proven practices become repeatable ways of working.
Replication: Successful practices move across LOBs, sites, and teams. 
Sustainability: Improvements remain after the initial intervention. 
Efficiency: Better processes reduce friction, waste, and unnecessary effort. 
Institutionalization: Knowledge, tools, routines, and capabilities become embedded. 

Governance question: Are we turning what works into how we operate?

CARL is not a scorecard BUT a governance lens through which the entire global account is led. 
Purpose
The business review is a single source of truth for the account. 

One deck must support:
Enterprise/C-suite review
Executive/LOB review
Global review
Site review
Operational review
TL execution discussions

The deck must allow leaders to move from: 

RESULT → VARIATION → DRIVER → ACTION → EFFECT → TRAJECTORY

The deck is not intended to make leaders read data aloud. 

Every slide MUST be understandable without the presenter. 
The Standard Slide Test
Every slide must pass three levels of comprehension
3-second Test
Looking only at the headline: Do I understand what happened?
30-second Test
Looking at the headline + visual: Do I understand why it happened and where?
3-Minute Test
Reading the supporting detail: Do I understand what we are doing and whether it is working?

If the answer to any of these questions is no, the slide needs to be redesigned. 
The Standard Slide Anatomy
Every analytical slide should contain four elements
Headline
A conclusion, not a topic

Do not write: CSAT Performance

Write: CSAT recovery is accelerating following the coaching intervention, but remains below quarterly target. 
Evidence
The chart, table, or visualization that proves the headline. 
SO, WHAT
One concise statement explaining the business implication. 
ACTION
What is being done, adjusted, continued, or escalated. 

Narrative should generally be limited to 1 headline + 2-3 supporting points. Avoid paragraphs. 
