SECTION 3 - KPI Trajectory
The purpose is not simply to display KPI performance. It MUST explain the movement over time. 
Required View:
Use a 13-week rolling trend with:
Actuals
Target
Major intervention markers - Is an action deliberately taken by the business that was substantial enough that we would reasonably expect it to influence the KPI. It is not every action in the log.
Examples:
New coaching methodology launched
Major process change
New QA rubric
Targeted performance interventions
Contact-routing change
Major policy update
Test question: Would we reasonably expect this intervention to change the KPI or its trajectory? If yes, mark it. If it’s a BAU activity like coaching or outlier management, don’t mark it. 
Material change markers - Is something that changed in the operating environment that wasn’t necessarily intended to improve the KPI
Examples:
Major policy change
Product/process change
New contact reason introduced
Significant contact mix change or channel mix
System/tool release
Major staffing composition change (new hire increase, mass removals of bottom performers etc.)
Significant volume change (concurrency change)
Organizational change
External event affecting contact volume
Key distinction:
Intervention - we changed something to influence performance
Material - Something changed in the environment that influenced performance
Important inflection points - Is when a KPI trajectory meaningfully changes direction or rate of movement. This is an observed performance event rather than something that was done.
Example: Smiley declines steadily for 6 weeks → begins improving rapidly in Week 8. 

Week 8 is an inflection point. 

The BIDS team will then ask, What happened around week 8? 

This is where the analytical story begins. 
The Three Together
The important part is that they don’t automatically imply causation. If an intervention happened in Week 8 and the KPI improved in Week 9, the deck should show both events but shouldn’t automatically say the intervention caused the improvement. 

