Continued Guidance For Slide 3
Relationship to KPI
This field answers: How strongly can we connect the event to the KPI movement? 

Classification
Definition
When to Use
Confirmed
Strong evidence that the event materially influenced the KPI
Timing, direction, mechanism and supporting data align; ideally validated by more than one source
Likely
Evidence strongly suggests the event influenced the KPI, but attribution is not fully proven
Event aligns with timing/direction and there is supporting operational evidence
Potential
Event could reasonably influence the KPI, but evidence is insufficient
Logical connection exists, but KPI movement is too early, mixed, or confounded
Unrelated
Evidence indicates the event did not materially influence the KPI
KPI moved independently or another driver explains the movement
Unknown
Relationship cannot currently be determined
Insufficient data/evidence


Important Rule: Do not use “Confirmed” simply because the event happened before the KPI moved. BIDS must look for:
		Timing + Direction + Mechanism + Evidence

Bad Example: QA, calibration launched → Quality Improves. 

Great Example: QA calibration on Context Gathering was launched in W-8 → Targeted markdown rate falls 22% in W-9 → 5 Target squads received the calibration → Context gathering improved 6.4% → Improvement was sustained in W-10 with an impact to overall QA score of 1.4%

Now, “Confirmed” becomes defensible. 
Event
A measurable performance movement or operating change that is large enough, unusual enough, or consequential enough to warrant investigation or documentation. 
This is critical because not every change in the KPI is an event. 

Example: Smiley moved from 80.1% → 80.3% → 80% → 80.2% should normally not generate four event markers. 
Recommended BPS Standard

For percentage based KPIs, use this guide: 

WoW Movement
Classification
Slide Treatment
<50 bps
Normal variation
No event marker
50-99 bps
Signal candidate
Investigate if unusual or repeated
100-199 bps
Material event
Event marker; investigate & take action
>200 bps
Major event
Prominent marker + narrative + red alert

Why 100 bps?
100 bps = 1.0 percentage point

For an 80% Smiley target:
79.9 → 19.7 = -20 bps → probably noise
80.0 → 79.5 = -50 bps → signal candidate
80.0 → 79.0 = -100 bps → material event
80.0 → 78.0 = -200 bps → major event
Caveat: 
Don’t use BPS alone. A fixed 100 bps threshold should be the default screening rule, not an absolute statistical truth. An event should also be considered material when it meets one of these conditions: 
Magnitude
>100 bps WOW
Persistence
>50 bps in the same direction for 2+ consecutive weeks. 
Example: -60 bps → -70 bps → -80 bps
Unusual Movement
The movement is materially outside the KPI’s normal historical variation. 
Example: A KPI normally moves +20 bps but suddenly moves -80 bps, that’s meaningful even though it hasn’t crossed 100 bps. 
Business Consequence
The movement is below the numerical threshold but creates meaningful business impact 
Example: Shopify Payment Payouts declines by only -40 bps but the taxonomy volume for this week was up by 2000+ tickets. This could be operationally material. 
Target/Threshold Breach
The movement causes the KPI to:
Cross below target
Cross above a risk threshold
Enter a defined performance band (penalty zone)
