Global Scalability Governance
A local action should only be recommended for global scaling when it meets the following principles. BIDS, QA, Training, Client Services and the Global Accounts Lead are the governing team responsible for deciding that a local action will be globally scaled. 
Proven Impact:
There is measurable improvement on the intended KPI or business outcome. There is enough evidence to distinguish the practice from concidence or unrelated environmental changes. 

	Action → measurable movement → sustained effect
Repeatability
The result isn’t dependent on one exceptional leader, team, or individual. The practice can be reasonably reproduced elsewhere. 
Transferability
The practice can work across different
LOBs
Sites
Channel
Tenure populations
Operating environments

Or, a clearly defined adaptation is required. 
Sustainability
The improvement survives beyond the initial intervention period. Avoid scaling something that worked for one week. 
Low Risk
The practice does not introduce unacceptable:
Compliance risks
Security risks
Merchant experience risks
People risks
Operational instability

